 **Review:** The AI suggests the MR title/body but does not open it. The developer opens the MR and pastes the URL. Session ends here — this is a genuine session boundary, not just a phase transition, since it waits on an out-of-band human approval.
 
 **Address Feedback:** Only runs if the approver raises concerns. The AI identifies which phase/step the concern traces to and suggests a named reiterate path (e.g. "Reiterate from Evaluate, Step 4"); it does not decide the fix unilaterally. The MR must be re-approved before Publish proceeds.
 
-**Publish:** The AI does not approve its own gate — a human must approve the MR out-of-band, with no unresolved concerns, before this phase runs. `{{DESIGN_ARCHITECT_AGENT}}` identifies `{{DESIGN_DOCS_ROOT}}` docs that need updating; `@docs-writer` applies the updates and `{{DESIGN_ARCHITECT_AGENT}}` reviews (max 2 passes). Archive and implementation-ticket creation happen after that review is clean. The design ticket's tracker status and effort are synced (worklog + transition to Done, delegated to `@scrum-master` by state name) as the final step.
+**Publish:** The AI does not approve its own gate — a human must approve the MR out-of-band, with no unresolved concerns, before this phase runs. `{{DESIGN_ARCHITECT_AGENT}}` identifies `{{DESIGN_DOCS_ROOT}}` docs that need updating; `@docs-writer` applies the updates and `{{DESIGN_ARCHITECT_AGENT}}` reviews (max 2 passes). After that review is clean, a single wrap-up stop asks one question — publish the implementation tickets from `Efforts.md` to the tracker? — then ticket publication (delegated to `@scrum-master` if yes), archive, and the design ticket's tracker sync (worklog effort derived from the design metrics + transition to Done, delegated to `@scrum-master` by state name) run straight through to a one-message close-out with the suggested commit.
 
